Let''s open tests.\\"}, {\\"role\\": \\"tool\\", \\"tool_call_id\\": \\"fc_342ebbf0-40f3-459c-b5a2-c73356e664ee\\", \\"content\\": \\"from solution import sum_range\\\\n\\\\n\\\\ndef test_small_range():\\\\n    assert sum_range(1, 4) == 10\\\\n\\\\n\\\\ndef test_single_value():\\\\n    assert sum_range(5, 5) == 5\\\\n\\"}, {\\"role\\": \\"assistant\\", \\"content\\": null, \\"refusal\\": null, \\"reasoning\\": \\"Only tests for small range and single value. But we should also consider negative ranges? Not needed. But we can implement robust. Let''s patch solution.py.\\", \\"reasoning_details\\": [{\\"index\\": 0, \\"type\\": \\"reasoning.text\\", \\"text\\": \\"Only tests for small range and single value. But we should also consider negative ranges? Not needed. But we can implement robust. Let''s patch solution.py.\\"}], \\"tool_calls\\": [{\\"index\\": 0, \\"type\\": \\"function\\", \\"id\\": \\"fc_67d654ee-8fcb-4623-9658-48ec16dc93bf\\", \\"function\\": {\\"name\\": \\"write_file\\", \\"arguments\\": \\"{\\\\\\"content\\\\\\":\\\\\\"def sum_range(start, end):\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Return the sum of all integers from start to end, both inclusive.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    The original implementation used ``range(start, end)`` which excludes the\\\\\\\\n    ``end`` value.  This caused the function to return the wrong result for\\\\\\\\n    ranges where ``start`` and ``end`` are different and for the trivial case\\\\\\\\n    where ``start == end``.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    The corrected implementation uses ``range(start, end + 1)`` so that the\\\\\\\\n    upper bound is included.  It also works correctly when ``start`` is\\\\\\\\n    greater than ``end`` by swapping the values \\\\u2013 this makes the function\\\\\\\\n    more robust and easier to reason about.\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    # Ensure the range is in ascending order.\\\\\\\\n    if start \\\\\\\\u003e end:\\\\\\\\n        start, end = end, start\\\\\\\\n    total = 0\\\\\\\\n    for n in range(start, end + 1):\\\\\\\\n        total += n\\\\\\\\n    return total\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\",\\\\\\"path\\\\\\":\\\\\\"solution.py\\\\\\"}\\"}}], \\"reasoning_content\\": \\"Only tests for small range and single value. But we should also consider negative ranges? Not needed. But we can implement robust. Let''s patch solution.py.\\"}, {\\"role\\": \\"tool\\", \\"tool_call_id\\": \\"fc_67d654ee-8fcb-4623-9658-48ec16dc93bf\\", \\"content\\": \\"{\\\\\\"ok\\\\\\": true, \\\\\\"path\\\\\\": \\\\\\"solution.py\\\\\\"}\\"}, {\\"role\\": \\"assistant\\", \\"content\\": null, \\"refusal\\": null, \\"reasoning\\": \\"Run tests.\\", \\"reasoning_details\\": [{\\"index\\": 0, \\"type\\": \\"reasoning.text\\", \\"text\\": \\"Run tests.\\"}], \\"tool_calls\\": [{\\"index\\": 0, \\"type\\": \\"function\\", \\"id\\": \\"fc_8551d5a4-ab93-4cf1-be2f-f492437462a7\\", \\"function\\": {\\"name\\": \\"run_tests\\", \\"arguments\\": \\"{}\\"}}], \\"reasoning_content\\": \\"Run tests.\\"}, {\\"role\\": \\"tool\\", \\"tool_call_id\\": \\"fc_8551d5a4-ab93-4cf1-be2f-f492437462a7\\", \\"content\\": \\"{\\\\\\"ok\\\\\\": true, \\\\\\"timed_out\\\\\\": false, \\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\": 0, \\\\\\"stdout\\\\\\": \\\\\\"..                                                                       [100%]\\\\\\\\n2 passed in 0.74s\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\", \\\\\\"stderr\\\\\\": \\\\\\"\\\\\\"}\\"}], \\"tools\\": [{\\"type\\": \\"function\\", \\"function\\": {\\"name\\": \\"list_files\\", \\"description\\": \\"List the files in the task workspace (relative paths).\\", \\"parameters\\": {\\"type\\": \\"object\\", \\"properties\\": {}, \\"additionalProperties\\": false}}}, {\\"type\\": \\"function\\", \\"function\\": {\\"name\\": \\"read_file\\", \\"description\\": \\"Read a UTF-8 file from the task workspace.\\", \\"parameters\\": {\\"type\\": \\"object\\", \\"properties\\": {\\"path\\": {\\"type\\": \\"string\\"}}, \\"required\\": [\\"path\\"], \\"additionalProperties\